WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.873
‘The system shall be scalable.’ That sentence is not yet a requirement.

2
00:00:07.873 --> 00:00:13.151
It names a concern and gives no measure, no load, and no behavior.

3
00:00:13.151 --> 00:00:22.896
This episode is about writing requirements that can fail a test — and about which kind of requirement actually pushes a team toward microservices.

4
00:00:23.496 --> 00:00:33.361
A functional requirement states what the system does: a customer can schedule a delivery; an order that has not been paid cannot be dispatched.

5
00:00:33.361 --> 00:00:36.238
The test is whether the behavior happened.

6
00:00:36.238 --> 00:00:43.636
A non-functional requirement states a quality of that behavior, or a constraint on the system that provides it

7
00:00:43.636 --> 00:00:51.446
— how fast, how available, how isolated between tenants, how hard to change, which region may hold the data.

8
00:00:51.446 --> 00:00:56.378
The test is whether the behavior met a measure. Both are requirements.

9
00:00:56.378 --> 00:01:00.900
A system can do the right thing and still be unfit.

10
00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:12.143
Take one feature: cancel a delivery. Functional: when a customer cancels before pickup, the delivery becomes cancelled and the customer is told.

11
00:01:12.143 --> 00:01:19.400
Functional, the refusal: after pickup, the delivery stays active and the customer is told why.

12
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:28.109
Non-functional, reliability: if the notification service is down, the cancellation still commits and the notice is sent later.

13
00:01:28.109 --> 00:01:33.431
Security: a token for tenant A cannot cancel tenant B’s delivery.

14
00:01:33.431 --> 00:01:39.720
Performance: at peak load, ninety-five percent of cancellations complete within the agreed time.

15
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:46.010
And a constraint: delivery data for a country stays in that country’s region.

16
00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:56.170
The first two rows are the business rule inside the aggregate. The rest are qualities of how that rule is offered.

17
00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:08.283
Functional requirements come from the work — EventStorming timelines, domain stories, given-when-then examples, the system operations Assemblage starts from.

18
00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:20.402
Non-functional requirements come from the conditions under which the work must succeed, and ISO/IEC 25010:2023 is their home: nine characteristics.

19
00:02:20.402 --> 00:02:25.855
Functional suitability is the quality of the functional side.

20
00:02:25.855 --> 00:02:38.581
The other eight — performance efficiency, compatibility, interaction capability, reliability, security, maintainability, flexibility, and safety — are where non-functional requirements live.

21
00:02:38.581 --> 00:02:44.640
The 2023 edition renamed usability and portability and added safety.

22
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.269
Here is the connection to everything before.

23
00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:59.521
A functional requirement can be met inside one service or across several; the existence of a function does not by itself force a new deployable unit.

24
00:02:59.521 --> 00:03:03.848
Non-functional requirements are what make the cut expensive or necessary.

25
00:03:03.848 --> 00:03:09.906
Independent change and a fast pipeline are maintainability and flexibility — Richardson’s dark energy.

26
00:03:09.906 --> 00:03:16.398
A local transaction and a latency budget are reliability and performance — his dark matter.

27
00:03:16.398 --> 00:03:22.889
Microservices are often chosen because a non-functional requirement cannot be met inside one release unit.

28
00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:34.140
They are often regretted because the non-functional cost of distribution — latency, partial failure, operability — was not written down as a requirement of its own.

29
00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:41.729
Bass, Clements, and Kazman treat a quality attribute as a six-part scenario: where the stimulus comes from,

30
00:03:41.729 --> 00:03:49.952
what it is, which part of the system it hits, the conditions at the time, the response, and the measure.

31
00:03:49.952 --> 00:03:56.941
Written that way, a requirement can fail. ‘When the payment service returns errors above the agreed threshold,

32
00:03:56.941 --> 00:04:06.397
the order service stops calling it and still accepts the order as pending within the agreed time’ — that is a circuit-breaker requirement.

33
00:04:06.397 --> 00:04:10.097
‘The system shall be resilient’ is a topic heading.

34
00:04:10.097 --> 00:04:15.853
The given-when-then example checks the rule; the scenario checks the quality under stated conditions.

35
00:04:16.453 --> 00:04:21.358
A generated endpoint that cancels a delivery has addressed a functional requirement.

36
00:04:21.358 --> 00:04:31.987
It has not addressed isolation, latency, or what happens when notification is down, unless those scenarios were in the request and show up in the design.

37
00:04:31.987 --> 00:04:35.666
Tools that emit code are practicing the functional side.

38
00:04:35.666 --> 00:04:43.434
Tools that choose a boundary, a region, a replica count, or a tenant placement are practicing the non-functional side.

39
00:04:43.434 --> 00:04:53.245
A suggestion to split a service is an answer to a non-functional requirement — and it is only justified if that requirement was measured.

40
00:04:53.845 --> 00:05:06.378
Three things to carry away. One. Functional: what it does. Non-functional: how well, under what constraints. Both must be testable. Two.

41
00:05:06.378 --> 00:05:18.910
Services are split to meet a non-functional requirement; the cost of splitting is also non-functional and must be written down. Three.

42
00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:25.475
Two test shapes: the given-when-then example, and the six-part quality-attribute scenario.

43
00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:36.018
Next: the discipline itself. Eighteen knowledge areas, thirty processes, and the difference between verification and validation.
